From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] A hidden navigation bar answers only a swipe up from the bottom edge

The navigation bar is shown by default on this model. The reader can put it away with
the bar's own key (patch 0077), which sets the Settings.Global entry
`readom_nav_locked` to 1 -- a literal name, not a new constant in `Settings.java`,
because a new constant would change framework.jar and the ART boot image with it and
every application in /data would be recompiled at the next upgrade. While it is 1 the
bar is hidden through `policy_control`, as before, and this patch decides what may
bring it back.

What went wrong in the first version, found on the reader's device: a swipe **down
from the top** revealed the navigation bar. `requestTransientBars()` computes
`sb = mStatusBarController.checkShowTransientBarLw()` and
`nb = mNavigationBarController.checkShowTransientBarLw()` and then shows whichever
is true, no matter which edge was swiped -- the top swipe asks for the status bar
and the navigation bar comes out with it ("the status bar and the navigation bar
appear together", the reader's own words). The guard the first version had only
refused requests whose `swipeTarget` was the navigation bar, so a top swipe walked
past it. `nb` now carries the same condition the transient-visibility decision
already had, and a hidden bar answers nothing but the reveal below.

The reveal itself, and how long it lasts:

  - a swipe up from the bottom edge, started within 24 dp of the edge and
    travelling 60 dp up within 800 ms. AOSP's own start threshold is the status bar
    height (24 dp here), so the edge is as easy to hit as the stock gesture; the
    travel is shorter than the first version's 120 dp, which a finger could not
    reach before leaving the edge;
  - the bar then stays up for 9 s, and a touch anywhere off the bar takes it away
    at once (the gesture listener now passes the touch position). It is shown as the
    real bar rather than AOSP's transient overlay, so its length is ours to decide;
  - the power-button panic gesture and the other transient requests keep their AOSP
    behaviour for the status bar; the navigation bar stays hidden while the reader
    has put it away (`transientNavBarAllowed`).

`BarController.hideTransientNow()` is the one new method on the policy side: a
transient bar cannot be hidden with `setBarShowingLw(false)` alone, because its own
state machine keeps it up until the request is denied.

Everything above is inert unless `readom_nav_locked` is 1, which only this model's
boot service writes; a build of this patch for PW3 behaves exactly as it does today.

Not run on a device yet when this patch was written; the directions to run are in
`readom-port/reports/t09-nav-impl.md`.

